# ---- 3.5 Hobbs volume ----
rep('// 4. 2000s\n',
    'children.push(H("3.5 The state of the field in 1993: the Hobbs volume", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));\n'
    'children.push(P("A useful snapshot of the field at the end of this era is the edited volume *Aerosol–Cloud–Climate Interactions* (Hobbs, 1993), which grew out of presentations at the 1991 IUGG assembly in Vienna. Its preface frames the subject as six interactions among aerosol, clouds and climate, \\"many of which we are only just beginning to understand, let alone incorporate into climate models\\" (Hobbs, 1993, Preface). Five of its chapters bear directly on this review, and each closes with a list of outstanding problems. Hobbs\'s own chapter concludes that ship tracks \\"well demonstrate\\" the potential effect of aerosol on cloud structure and radiative properties, but that \\"the regional and global effects of aerosol on cloud radiative properties remain uncertain\\"; that a global inventory of the principal sources and nature of CCN is required; that the role of organics in CCN formation \\"is potentially important but has not been quantified\\"; that long time-series of CCN spectra are needed at a variety of locations; and that the role of clouds as a source of aerosol and CCN is potentially of global importance but \\"not yet settled\\" (Hobbs, 1993, Ch. 2). The same chapter records the then-current status of the DMS–cloud–climate (CLAW) hypothesis, noting that whether DMS emissions increase with temperature \\"is not known\\", and remarks that \\"too many theories are chasing too few measurements\\" (Hobbs, 1993, Ch. 2)."));\n'
    'children.push(P("Harshvardhan\'s chapter on aerosol–climate interactions judged the direct forcing to be known \\"to within a factor of 2 or 3\\" but stated that \\"the same cannot be said of the indirect forcing through changes in cloud microphysics\\". It identified the primary hurdle as the modeling of cloud feedbacks, noting that the range of cloud shortwave forcing among the general circulation models of the day exceeded the total aerosol forcing by an order of magnitude, and concluded that the success of any attempt to model or monitor aerosol effects \\"will ultimately rest on our ability to isolate the aerosol signal from the much more dominant effect of changes in cloudiness\\" (Harshvardhan, 1993). King\'s chapter on the radiative properties of clouds flagged the \\"anomalous absorption paradox\\" (aircraft measurements consistently suggesting more solar absorption by clouds than theory predicts for liquid water and water vapor) as a four-decade-old discrepancy, while noting that the bulk of the then-recent evidence pointed to water-vapor absorption in the near-infrared window regions rather than to the droplets; it closed with the observations that there had been \\"a longstanding tendency for too many theories to chase too few observations\\", that spectral and angular measurements with collocated microphysics were the way forward, and that retrieval schemes based on Mie theory and plane-parallel radiative transfer had not yet been extended to horizontally inhomogeneous clouds (King, 1993). Heymsfield\'s chapter listed, among the measurements needed before the effects of stratiform clouds on climate could be evaluated realistically, quantitative criteria for defining cloud types, CCN concentrations as a function of geographic location and altitude, and droplet size distributions and liquid water contents in stratus and stratocumulus \\"for a wide range of geographical areas\\" (Heymsfield, 1993). Jaenicke\'s chapter on tropospheric aerosol emphasized the extreme spatial and temporal variability of the aerosol, the lack of standardized and intercompared instrumentation, the question of whether \\"sulfate aerosols, in the strict sense of the term, exist, or are all aerosols mixed in nature\\", and the need for long time series obtained \\"mainly for basic understanding\\" rather than for monitoring alone (Jaenicke, 1993). Sundqvist\'s chapter on cloud parameterization noted that only vertically integrated cloud water could then be measured and that knowledge of how to parameterize cloud cover was \\"regrettably poor\\" (Sundqvist, 1993). Section 9.2 returns to each of these problems and asks which have since been answered."));\n'
    '\n// 4. 2000s\n')

