children.push(P("Two conclusions follow from the table. First, the problems that have been resolved are almost all problems of infrastructure or of a specific falsifiable hypothesis: global aerosol monitoring now exists, the CLAW hypothesis was tested and retired, and the anomalous-absorption discrepancy shrank once better experiments and spectroscopy were available. Second, the problems that remain open are the ones that were already recognized in 1993 as problems of attribution rather than of measurement: separating the aerosol signal from cloud variability, quantifying the global CCN budget and its organic and mixing-state controls, and constraining the cloud-fraction response. Hobbs's remark that too many theories were chasing too few measurements has, for warm clouds, been inverted: the satellite era produced measurements faster than the field could agree on how to interpret them (McComiskey and Feingold, 2012; Quaas et al., 2020). The following questions carry the open 1993 problems into the present list."));

children.push(Num("The global and regional radiative effect of aerosol on clouds (Hobbs, 1993, Ch. 2; Harshvardhan, 1993). The question as posed in 1993 is still the headline question of the field: ERFaci is bounded but its 68% range spans a factor of about three (Bellouin et al., 2020; Forster et al., 2021). The modern form of the question is whether regime- and morphology-conditioned susceptibilities, aggregated with observed regime frequencies, can narrow that range (Goren et al., 2026), and whether ground-based sites such as EPCAPE can supply the per-regime values."));
children.push(Num("Isolating the aerosol signal from cloudiness variability (Harshvardhan, 1993). This is the same problem as question 2 above, posed thirty years earlier. Its persistence suggests that it will not be settled by larger data sets alone; it requires experimental designs in which meteorology is controlled or explicitly modeled (Gryspeerdt et al., 2016; Goren et al., 2025; Christensen et al., 2022). For a single-site record, the open question is which conditioning variables (coupling state, LWP, inversion strength, free-tropospheric humidity, cloud morphology) are sufficient to remove the confounding."));
children.push(Num("The CCN budget and its organic and mixing-state controls (Hobbs, 1993, Ch. 2; Jaenicke, 1993). Over most of the ocean, fewer than 30% of CCN are sea spray and the remainder is mainly sulfate entrained from the free troposphere (Quinn et al., 2017); organic enrichment of marine particles is established (Russell et al., 2010) but its net effect on CCN, and the effect of mixing state, remain to be integrated into models (Riemer et al., 2019). At a coastal urban site such as EPCAPE, the open question is what fraction of the CCN that actually reach cloud base in each regime is marine, free-tropospheric or urban, and whether that partition changes the Twomey response (Dedrick et al., 2026)."));
children.push(Num("Clouds as a source of aerosol (Hobbs, 1993, Ch. 2). Convective outflow is now known to be a major source of new particles that grow to CCN (Williamson et al., 2019), but the global source strength is unquantified and the role of non-precipitating stratocumulus, beyond the Hoppel-minimum processing that EPCAPE analyses already use (Hoppel et al., 1994; Dedrick et al., 2026), has not been established. Can the post-cloud aerosol signature in surface size distributions and in AERONET-type spectral retrievals (Eck et al., 2012) be used to quantify in-cloud production at a stratocumulus site?"));
children.push(Num("Vertical distribution of CCN and its connection to cloud base (Heymsfield, 1993). The vertical distribution above the boundary layer remains the least-sampled part of the CCN climatology, and decoupling can remove most accumulation-mode particles from the cloud layer (Wang et al., 2021, 2026). This is the 1993 form of question 6 above: what is the right way to map surface aerosol to cloud-base aerosol in a partially coupled boundary layer?"));
children.push(Num("The cloud-fraction adjustment (Sundqvist, 1993; Hobbs, 1993, Ch. 2). Cloud-cover parameterization was judged the weakest element of cloud modeling in 1993, and the aerosol effect on cloud fraction is still the least constrained adjustment (Gryspeerdt et al., 2016; Christensen et al., 2022). For a ground-based site the question is whether cloud-fraction and cloud-lifetime responses can be observed at all from a fixed point, or only inferred from the cloud-field morphology seen by satellite overpasses."));
children.push(Num("Horizontal inhomogeneity in radiation and retrievals (King, 1993). Three-dimensional radiative transfer is understood (Marshak and Davis, 2005) and its surface-irradiance consequences in broken cloud are large (Gristey et al., 2020), but the retrievals that ACI studies rely on, including the ground-based ones used at EPCAPE, remain plane-parallel. For overcast stratocumulus the bias may be small; the open question is how small, and whether it is correlated with aerosol loading through cloud morphology."));
children.push(Num("Spectral radiation with collocated microphysics (King, 1993). King's prescription, spectral and angular radiation measurements interpreted with simultaneous cloud microphysics, has been realized on aircraft and from space (McBride et al., 2011; Stephens et al., 2018) but only in short campaigns at the surface. SCARPE at EPCAPE (Lubin et al., 2026) is one such data set; whether a one-year record of this kind is sufficient to detect an aerosol signal in spectral transmission, rather than only to test closure, is the question that Sections 7 and 9.1 (questions 8 and 9) pose."));
